{"kind":"request","text":"Design: a diff a person can read in the card\n\nScope: Hugh's reaction to the J2 session, at 14:20 EST, with `~/play/artroom-evidence/2026-11-01-j2-person-3/2-diff.png`: \"The diff UI looks fine, and also the 'view file' UI works well. But I'm concerned about readability of the display area - without being collapsible the diff and display can get very large; and visually the various parts of the diff are quite difficult to distinguish; we may be better to look at current state-of-the-art visual diff tools to find a better UI approach.\"\n\nThe scene: a reviewer opens a change with ten files in the desktop AI app. They see what changed in a few lines, without scrolling past commit IDs and prose. They open the file that matters. Additions, removals, context and hunk headers read apart at a glance, and they can tell the change's own edits from what the new base brought. The exact commits stay one press away, and no answer hides what it left out.\n\nWhat the screenshot shows today: one `pre.diff` block of monochrome text. The four commits print in the band and again in the text. The summary is prose. `---`, `+++` and `@@` lines look like the code. Nothing collapses. Your J2 note says the design packet's gutters and line colours were left out, so read `notes/2026-10-17-journey-views-design/renders/file-diff-dark-1536.png` and `file-interdiff-dark-1536.png` first. Earlier feedback on these cards stands: a small frame, one thing at a time, and minimal narrative labels.\n\nWrite a design note, with no code built:\n1. Survey what current tools do for the problems above, by using them or by reading their own documentation. Keep the survey in the evidence unit. The note says what we chose and why, with no comparisons, because it is public. Cover at least: a change summary, per-file collapse, collapsed unchanged context with expansion, unified against split at chat width, word-level highlighting, line gutters, and how interdiff tools show a rebase. A first lead: `@pierre/diffs`, Apache-2.0, with `PatchDiff` from a patch string, vanilla JS and Shadow DOM.\n2. Decide between our own small renderer and a vendored library. Measure, do not estimate. The card holds a bound of 1 MiB per resource, its CSP allows no outside script, and that package unpacks to about 11 MB, so its built size is the question. Syntax highlighting is optional: say what it costs and whether it earns that.\n3. Say what the server must send. Today it sends text. A renderer may need structured files, hunks and groups, and an answer's size counts toward the frame.\n4. Keep what the checker approved: each statement of a group under-claims, \"Truncated\" and what it left out stay visible, and collapsed text is not the same as omitted text. A collapsed file must say so.\n5. Show it: rendered mockups at chat width and narrow width, in both themes, of a ten-file diff, the interdiff with a new base, and a truncated diff, on real answers from the J2 room. Render them in the real card frame if you can.\n\nDone when the checker approves and the note lands. Then I show Hugh the renders and he decides before any build. This does not reopen J2's acceptance.\n\nOrder: after the press timeout 71d50311 and J5's acceptance.","body":{"conditions":"A design note on a readable, collapsible diff in the card, with a measured renderer decision and rendered mockups on real answers, approved by the checker and landed on main, with no code built.","target_head":"0719adce30649c41479764ffdddf1dab1fd218ed","target_ref":"refs/heads/main","target_repo":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3","to":"feb90d36627c7f4466ef9473c4fa0bbaf91699d16b93d832a37629988dfba6de"}}